= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Work with a nonsingular <multivariate normal distribution>, so $V$ is <positive definite>. The claimed finite <maximum likelihood estimates> also require the centered <sample covariance matrix> to be nonsingular; the exceptional case is discussed below.

Independence gives, up to a constant, the <log-likelihood>
$$
\ell(\mu,V)=-\frac n2\log\det V
-\frac12\sum_{i=1}^n(Y_i-\mu)^TV^{-1}(Y_i-\mu).
$$
Let $\bar Y=n^{-1}\sum_iY_i$ and $S=n^{-1}\sum_i(Y_i-\bar Y)(Y_i-\bar Y)^T$. The centered sum is zero, and expansion therefore gives
$$
\sum_i(Y_i-\mu)(Y_i-\mu)^T
=nS+n(\bar Y-\mu)(\bar Y-\mu)^T.
$$
For every fixed <positive-definite matrix> $V$, the extra quadratic term in the <log-likelihood> is $-n(\bar Y-\mu)^TV^{-1}(\bar Y-\mu)/2$, with unique maximum at $\mu=\bar Y$.

Assume $S$ is <positive definite>. Maximizing over $V$ now means minimizing $\log\det V+\operatorname{tr}(V^{-1}S)$. Put $A=S^{1/2}V^{-1}S^{1/2}$, another <positive-definite matrix>. Then
$$
\log\det V+\operatorname{tr}(V^{-1}S)
=\log\det S-\log\det A+\operatorname{tr}A.
$$
If $a_1,\ldots,a_p>0$ are its <eigenvalues>, the variable part is $\sum_j(a_j-\log a_j)$. The scalar inequality $a-\log a\ge1$, with equality only at $a=1$, shows that this is minimized uniquely at $A=I$, or $V=S$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\widehat\mu=\bar Y,\qquad
\widehat V=S=\frac1n\sum_i(Y_i-\bar Y)(Y_i-\bar Y)^T.}
$$
The divisor is $n$ because this is <maximum likelihood estimation>, not the unbiased <covariance> estimate with divisor $n-1$.

The nonsingularity condition matters. The centered scatter has <rank> at most $n-1$. If $S$ is singular, choose $\mu=\bar Y$, retain positive <covariance> <eigenvalues> on its range and let a <covariance> <eigenvalue> on its <null space> tend to zero. The quadratic <likelihood> term remains finite, while $-\log\det V\to+\infty$. This is <singular covariance and nonexistence of a Gaussian maximum likelihood estimate>: there is no finite maximizer over nonsingular $V$. Under a nonsingular <Gaussian> population, $S$ is positive definite almost surely when $n>p$; for $n\le p$ it is necessarily singular. The asserted estimate and the inverse in the next part have their usual meaning under $n>p$.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

By the <spectral theorem>, write $V=U\operatorname{diag}(v_1,\ldots,v_p)U^T$, where $U$ is <orthogonal> and each $v_j>0$. Choose the <whitening transformation>
$$
\boxed{L=V^{-1/2}=U\operatorname{diag}(v_1^{-1/2},\ldots,v_p^{-1/2})U^T.}
$$
A <linear image of a multivariate normal vector> is <multivariate normal>, and $LVL^T=I$. Thus $Z_i=L(Y_i-\mu)$ are independent $N_p(0,I)$ <random vectors>. Their whole joint distribution is independent of $\mu,V$.

Define $\bar Z=n^{-1}\sum_iZ_i$ and $S_Z=n^{-1}\sum_i(Z_i-\bar Z)(Z_i-\bar Z)^T$. These satisfy
$$
\bar Z=L(\bar Y-\mu),\qquad S_Z=LSL^T,
\qquad S_Z^{-1}=L^{-T}S^{-1}L^{-1}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{(\bar Y-\mu)^TS^{-1}(\bar Y-\mu)
=\bar Z^TS_Z^{-1}\bar Z.}
$$
The right side is a function of independent standard <multivariate normal> observations only, so its distribution depends on $n,p$ but not on $\mu,V$. This is the same <affine invariance of Hotelling's statistic> that permits multivariate mean tests with unknown <covariance>. The proof concerns the jointly transformed mean and <covariance>; whitening the mean alone would not establish the conclusion. Inverses exist almost surely under $n>p$. If $V$ is singular, no square <matrix> can whiten it to $I$, so nonsingularity of the population <covariance> is necessary as well.

